1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inglis_Palgrave:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Harry Inglis Palgrave, FRS, FSS (11 June 1827 – 25 January 1919) was a British economist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Harry Inglis Palgrave was born on 11 June 1827. He was the son of Francis Palgrave and his wife Elizabeth Turner, daughter of the banker Dawson Turner. His brothers were Francis Turner Palgrave, William Gifford Palgrave and
2 CONC  Sir Reginald Palgrave.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at Charterhouse School and the University of Cambridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1843, at the age of 16, he joined the bank of Deacon, Williams and Co. He then in 1845 joined Dawson Turner Turner and Gurney in Yarmouth, the banking firm of his grandfather Dawson Turner. He was also a director of Barclay and
2 CONC  Co. and a clerk of the House of Commons.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1877 he became financial editor of The Economist and became editor-in-chief on the death of Walter Bagehot the same year, a position he held until 1883. He produced the three volume Palgrave's Dictionary of Political Economy (1
2 CONC 894, 1896 and 1899) and also edited the collected historical works of his father, Sir Francis Palgrave. He also edited The Banking Almanac until his death, and for a time was editor of The Bankers’ Magazine.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in June 1882.[2] He was knighted in 1909 on the final completion of his dictionary.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1859 Sarah Maria Brightwen, daughter of George Brightwen of Saffron Walden.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on 25 January 1919.
