2 CONT
2 CONT For correspondence between the Astronomer-Royal, Sir John Frederick William Herschel, and FM Eardley-Wilmot, see: http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/A2A/records.aspx?cat=117-jh_1-2&cid=-1#-1
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://www.english-heritage.org.uk/content/imported-docs/u-z/woolwich-chap7.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT The Royal Artillery Institute (later Institution) was formed in 1838 as an educational scientific club for artillery officers. The Regiment had always been scientific, but this was also a time when Howick’s reforms had put educati
2 CONC on to the fore. The Institute was founded by two young officers, Lt. (later Gen. Sir) John Lefroy and Lt. (later Maj.-Gen.) Frederick Marow Eardley-Wilmot, the former inspired by the precedent of Charles Hutton’s short-lived Milit
2 CONC ary Society of the 1770s. The Institute’s first building of 1838–9, erected under the supervision of Lt. Col. George Harding, CRE, was an observatory, on high ground near the Rotunda, for training in magnetic observations, to assi
2 CONC st in a pioneering global survey of the 1840s with which Lefroy and Eardley-Wilmot were closely involved. For its telescopes it had a transit room, flat roofed with paired tall thin windows due north and south for observations, an
2 CONC d, further west, a small round equatorial room with a conical roof. On the other side of its entrance lobby to the east a three-bay single-storey and basement block housed a library and, to the north, a reading and writing room. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eardley Bryan provides this information: 
2 CONT
2 CONT In front of the Old Building at RMA Sandhurst are some Blomefield pattern cannons and 'F M Eardley-Wilmot' is stamped on some of them. His great-uncle Sir Thomas Blomefield (http://www.geni.com/people/Sir-Thomas-Blomefield-1st-Bt/
2 CONC 6000000002427749500?through=6000000002427499175) was previously Inspector General of Ordnance. Frederick Eardley-Wilmot was Superintendent of the Gun Factories from 1855.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nature 30, 285-287 (24 July 1884) | doi:10.1038/030285a0
2 CONT
2 CONT The Construction of Ordnance
2 CONT
2 CONT Abstract
2 CONT
2 CONT ABOUT thirty years ago, during the raging of the Crimean war, special attention began to be directed towards the improvement of our artillery. The old Board of Ordnance was abolished. The manufacturing departments at Woolwich were
2 CONC  put under the control of a newly created Minister of War. That able and high-minded officer, Colonel F. M. Eardley-Wilmot, R.A., was appointed Superintendent of the Gun Factories, July 1855. He commenced his work in a thoroughly 
2 CONC sensible and practical manner, and pursued his inquiries for suitable materials for guns both at home and abroad. He was ready to adopt anything, new or old, provided it was of the right sort. Sir H. Bessemer has remarked: “My ear
2 CONC ly progress was known to only a few scientific men, among whom was Colonel Eardley-Wilmot, R. A., who took great interest in the invention.” 
