2 CONT * Edward Parry, head, married, male, aged 51 [born about 1830], in New South Wales; Bishop of Dover
2 CONT * Matilda Parry, wife, married, female, aged 49 [born about 1832], in Kensington, Middlesex; Bishop's Wife
2 CONT * Edward Archibald Parry, son, single, male, aged 21 [born about 1860], in Acton, Middlesex; Student
2 CONT * Frederick Sydney Parry, son, single, male, aged 19 [born about 1862], in Acton, Middlesex; Student
2 CONT * Charles Parry, son, single, male, aged 7 [born about 1874], in Canterbury, Kent; Scholar
2 CONT * Isabel Emmeline Parry, daughter, single, female, aged 12 [born about 1869], in Acton, Middlesex; Scholar
2 CONT * Bertha Matilda Parry, daughter, single, female, aged 10 [born about 1871], in Canterbury, Kent; Scholar
2 CONT * William Rogers, servant, married, male, aged 36 [born about 1845], in Bergh Apton, Norfolk; Butler
2 CONT * Harriet Child, servant, single, female, aged 46 [born about 1835], in Ringland, Norfolk; Nurse
2 CONT * Caroline Collins, servant, single, female, aged 33 [born about 1848], in Swanage, Dorset; Cook
2 CONT * Mary Elizabeth Penfold, servant, single, female, aged 20 [born about 1861], in Honington, Kent; Housemaid
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=PSiPR9uNGovvhZm%2BZ%2FgCqg&scan=1 FreeBMD: Registration of death of Edward Parry in 1890]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT April to June 1890: Registration of death of Edward Parry; aged 60 [born about 1830]; in Hastings (Volume 2b, Page 17)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0002464/18900412/088/0006 British Newspaper Archive: Middlesex County Times Saturday, 12 April 1890 Page 6 Death of the Bishop of Dover]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT We regret to record the death of the Bishop of Dover, which sad event took place at St Leonards-on-Sea at 4 o'clock yesterday (Friday 11 April 1890) morning.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the space of nearly 40 years the deceased (Dr. Parry) was well and favourably known in the diocese of London, in which he was an active worker. As far back as 1857 he became Chaplain to the Bishop of London (Dr. Tait), whose w
2 CONC arm friendship he never lost, and became with the clergy and laity extremely popular in every part of the diocese of Canterbury.
2 CONT
2 CONT ln Middlesex he was better known, and his high Christian qualities appreciated, as Rector of Acton. This position Dr. Parry held for 10 years — from 1859 to 1869. He was also Rural Dean of Ealing from 1863 to 1869, his appointment
2 CONC  as Archdeacon and Canon of Canterbury dating from the latter year. During this period Dr. Parry lost no opportunity for becoming acquainted with matters affecting the diocese, his frank, generous character and contagious genialit
2 CONC y winning for him the friendship of all with whom, he was brought into contact.
2 CONT
2 CONT Speaking of his personal qualities, one writer says truthfully that he was a capital host, and at two meetings of the Pan-Anglican Conference during his term of office, Dr. Parry entertained the American and Colonial Bishops in a 
2 CONC way that, as the poet-prelate of the United States (Bishop Cleveland Coxe) said, "Put us all at our ease in a moment." 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was very popular at school, at college, as a tutor at Durham, and in all his after life. Open and straightforward, he detested falsehood or prevarication. He had many of the qualities of a sailor, and was devoted to the sea; an
2 CONC d no one brought into contact with his genial face, and who felt his hearty handshake, could fail to implicitly trust him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a busy life, and his death at the present moment will be nowhere more lamented than in the Rural Deanery of Ealing. Twenty years ago Dr. Parry was appointed Suffragan Bishop of Dover, a position from which he retired last y
2 CONC ear, when compelled by failing health.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his career he took a considerable part in the proceedings of the Lower House of Convocation, and was a great authority on the history of the Cathedral, or the adjacent college of St. Augustine, at Canterbury, and the many t
2 CONC reasures they contained.
2 CONT
2 CONT But few had reason to anticipate the end was so near, notwithstanding that Dr. Parry had not for same considerable period enjoyed robed health. The primary complaint from which he suffered was the cardiac affection with which he w
2 CONC as attacked some few months ago. His condition last week was alarming, and on Good Friday the members of his family were summoned. The present week, however, brought a slight change, as it was thought for the better, but unfortuna
2 CONC tely this improvement was not maintained, and he succumbed at an early hour yesterday.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mother of Dr. Parry was a daughter of the first Lord Stanley of Alderley. The deceased was Commissary in England of the Bishop of Gibraltar, and honorary chaplain of the 1st Battalion East Kent Volunteers. He was a member of t
2 CONC he Athenaeum Club.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''His Career''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Right Rev. Edward Parry, D.D., says Men of the Time, was the only surviving son of the late Rear Admiral Sir Edward Parry, K.C.B., the well-known navigator and explorer of the Arctic regions. He was born at Government House, S
2 CONC ydney, New South Wales, in 1830, and after a preliminary training at Rugby School, entered Balliol College, Oxford, in 1849, graduating B.A. (first-class in classics) in 1852, and M.A. in 1855. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1853 to 1856 he was tutor of Durham University. He was ordained deacon in 1854, priest in 1855, and in 1856 he held the curacy of Sonning, Berkshire, under the Rev. Hugh Pearson. On the elevation of Dr. Tait to the See of Lon
2 CONC don, at the close of that year, Mr. Parry became his domestic chaplain, residing and working with his Lordship for nearly three years. In the ten years which followed, from 1859 to 1869, he held the rectory of Acton, Middlesex, an
2 CONC d took an active past in all London diocesan matters. He was also Rural Dean of Ealing from 1863 to 1869, when he was appointed Archdeacon and Canon of Canterbury. ln 1870, he was appointed Bishop Suffragan of Dover, for the Dioce
2 CONC se of Canterbury, being consecrated in the chapel of Lambeth Palace on March 25th, under letters patent from the Queen and a commission from the Primate. It is worthy of note that he and the Bishop Suffragan of Nottingham (Mackens
2 CONC ie), in the same year, were the first Suffragan Bishops consecrated in the Anglican Church for nearly 300 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1882 he was elected by the Australian bishops to the Bishopric of Sidney, as Metropolitan of Australia and Tasmania, but he declined the nomination. Bishop Parry has written memoirs of his father, a work which has passed throug
2 CONC h several editions, and "Memorials of (his brother) Commander Parry, R.N., 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/213389633?searchTerm=%22Right%20Reverend%20Edward%20Parry%22 The Daily Northern Argus (Rockhampton, Queensland) Monday, 14 April 1890 Page 3 Obituary: London, April 13. Bishop Pa
2 CONC rry.]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Right Reverend Edward Parry, D.D., was appointed Suffragan Bishop of Dover in 1870. The deceased was the son of the famous Arctic Explorer, Rear-Admiral Sir Edward Parry, K.C.B., and was born at Sydney in 1830. He and Bishop M
2 CONC cKenzie, of Nottingham, were the first Suffragan Bishops consecrated in the Anglican Church for nearly 300 years. Bishop Parry declined the Bishopric of Sydney, which was offered to him in 1882. He wrote the memoirs of his father,
2 CONC  which have passed through several editions.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Twin brother of Isabella Parry, born in Government House, Sydney, New South Wales, Australia, to  William Parry, Arctic explorer, and Isabella Louisa, his wife (fourth daughter of the John Stanley, 1st Baron Stanley of Alderney).<
2 CONC br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Matilda Williams on 18 May 1859 at Saint James, Westminster, London, England<br/>
