1 NAME Stuart /Eardley-Wilmot/
2 GIVN Stuart
2 _MARNM Eardley-Wilmot
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE SEP 1847
2 ADDR
3 CITY Hobart
3 STAE Tasmania
3 CTRY Australia
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 29 JUN 1931
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mr Stuart Eardley-Wilmot died at Lauceston, at the age of 85. Born in Hobart Stuart Eardley-Wilmot was the second son of Mr Augustus H Eardley-Wilmot. His mother was a daughter of the late Mr John Dunn, founder of 
2 CONC the Commercial Bank of Van Diemens Land (as it was then called), and his grandfather, Sir John E Eardley-Wilmot was governor of the colony from 1843 to 1846. Stuart E-W was educated in England till 1863, when he went to Queensland
2 CONC  to a catlle station and spent the years till 1869 there and in New South Wales in various stations. He had many exciting adventures in the then new colony of Queensland. In April 1869 he came to Launceston joining the staff of th
2 CONC e Commercial Bank, a couple of years later he entered into partnership with Mr John S Taylor in the wool and grain business. He married a daughter of Mr William Johnstone in 1874. Stuart E-W joined his brother in law Mr W Johnston
2 CONC e in June 1874 and carrying on the old establish business. The firm which was started in 1842 was altered to Johnstone and Wilmot. Mr Stuart E-W had over half a century of business life in Launceston and saw many changes, he and h
2 CONC is partner Mr W Johnstone did a whole of their own travelling for business for 17 years, when the north west coast was a very different place from what it is now. It was impossible to get from the coast to Launceston in a wheeled 
2 CONC vehicle and people had to ride all the way to Circular head. He saw the beginnings of Mount Bischoff and the east coast tin mines when travelling in that dangerous tedious region. He sat on the marine board for 15 years retiring w
2 CONC hen the franchise was changed to that of the house of assembly. He sat on the board of the launceston gas company for some years, also on the board of the mount Bischoff company for 15 years. He was of the executive committee of t
2 CONC he Launceston Bank for Savings and one of the commissioners for the sinking fund of the Launceston Municipal Corporation. Stuart E-W was on board the steemer Great Easten before she was launched and he saw the first atlantic cable
2 CONC  being towed on board HMS Agamemnon at Greenwich. He founded a branch of the Navy league in Launceston in 1900, and later years was able to get branchs started in Melbourne, Brisbane and Sydney. He sat on the board of the old Corn
2 CONC  Wall insurance company and most successful institution which was eventually sold out to the Commercial Union Insurance Company. Stuart E-W was chief representitive of the Northern Insurance Co. Ltd for a number of years and was t
2 CONC he oldest agent of the company on its books in point of service. There were three sons, Commander Trevor Eardley-Wilmot of Launceston, Mr Arthur Parry of Western Australia and Gerald Stuart Eardley-Wilmot. 
