1 OCCU Clerk in H.M. Treasury; Deputy Chairman H.M. Customs and Excise
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_Sydney_Parry Wikipedia - Frederick Sydney Parry]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Frederick Sydney Parry, KBE, CB (5 June 1861 – 22 May 1941) was a senior British civil servant, serving as deputy chairman of the Board of Customs from 1904 to 1909, and of the Board of Customs and Excise from 1909 to 1925
2 CONT
2 CONT Born on 5 June 1861, the second son of E. Parry, Bishop Suffragan of Dover, Frederick Sydney Parry was educated at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winchester_College Winchester College, College Street, Winchester, Hampshire SO23 9N
2 CONC A 51.05806, -1.31278] and at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balliol_College,_Oxford Balliol College, Broad Street, Oxford OX1 3BJ 51.7547, -1.2578]. In 1885 he was appointed to the Board of Trade, but the next year transferred to 
2 CONC the Treasury and became in 1888 assistant private secretary to the Chancellor of the Exchequer, which post he retained until 1892. From 1897 to 1892 he was private secretary to the First Lord of the Treasury. He was appointed a Co
2 CONC mpanion of the Order of the Bath (CB) in the 1902 Coronation Honours list on 26 June 1902, and in July that year was appointed a private secretary to the new Prime Minister, Alfred Balfour. Only three months later, he transferred 
2 CONC to the Treasury, where he had been promoted to Principal Clerk.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created a Knight Commander of the Order of the British Empire (KBE) in 1925.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parry married a daughter of Dean Fremantle, of Ripon, and had a son, Admiral Sir William Edward Parry KCB and a daughter, Katherine Parry (born 1895).
2 CONT
2 CONT Parry died at Hove on 22 May 1941.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/information.pl?cite=N48%2Bt5QSGDTULDqOnga6zg&scan=1 FreeBMD - Registration of birth of Frederick Sydney Parry in 1861]'''
