1 OCCU Admiral, Royal Navy
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Coote_(Royal_Navy_officer):
2 CONT
2 CONT Admiral Robert Coote CB (1 June 1820 – 17 March 1898) was a Royal Navy officer who went on to be Commander-in-Chief, China Station.
2 CONT
2 CONT Educated at Eton College,[2] Coote joined the Royal Navy in 1833 and served on the coast of Syria in 1840. He was made Commander of the HMS Volcano in 1851 while serving in the West Africa Squadron. Promoted to Captain in 1854, he
2 CONC  commanded HMS Victory from 1860, HMS Gibraltar from 1864 and HMS Arethusa from 1867. He became Commander-in-Chief, Queenstown in 1874 and Commander-in-Chief, China Station in 1878. He retired in 1885.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coote married Lucy Parry, daughter of the Arctic explorer Admiral Sir William Parry, in 1854. They had one son, Stanley Victor Coote, High Sheriff of Roscommon in 1900, and a daughter, Caroline Maud Coote, who married Major-Genera
2 CONC l Cecil William Park. Coote died in March 1898, aged 77. His wife died in February 1906.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in Woking Cemetery. There is a memorial to him in St Catherine's Church in Tullamore in County Offaly.
