2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE The disappearing and reappearing smart matches have happened for me before, and i thank you for contacting tech support. I am adding the names here to the photo, so it will show up in which of their media folders. 
2 NOTE Cat, this is quite strange - the SmartMatches have disappeared again.  I will contact tech support.
2 NOTE Cat, the SmartMatches reappeared.  This has happened before - confirming a SmartMatch, it disappearing altogether for a while, and then reappearing.  I guess some sort of tech-lag thing.
3 CONT
3 CONT I entered all the names on the tree.  Are you positive they aren't coming up for you?
3 CONT
3 CONT The handwriting looks like it could be my mother's, but different enough from what I remember - perhaps because she would have here been printing carefully - for me to not be certain. 
3 CONT
3 CONT By the way - and totally separately - in fixing stuff up in this part of the tree I learned that Elisabeth Ettlinger - who is on the chart on the left - was an archaeologist, specializing, from what I can tell, in Provincial Rome.
3 CONC   Interesting.
2 NOTE Thanks for the further information. I see what you mean about the dates and address not being of the same era. I know these are on your / our maternal line, but Fritz seems to have been interested in genealogy and history generall
3 CONC y. Do you recognize the handwriting? If is very neat and seems to have been prepared with ease of copying in mind. Have you entered all of the names in yet. I have set myself to "follow" all of those listed, but not all of them ar
3 CONC e coming up here (yet). I don't know about the missing smart matches. That is something i have noticed / not noticed at various times. I refuse to purchase a MyHeritage account, so i take what i can get when i can get it. Perhaps 
3 CONC a curator such as Randy ot Thomas may be of help here. 
2 NOTE Hi Cat, yes I noticed "Western Germany" which indicates a period from the end of the war perhaps up to the establishment of the FRG in 1949.  However, this document was prepared no earlier than 1965.  Look at the noted year of dea
3 CONC th for the elder Fritz Brinkmann on the chart.  Also, you can see in the upper right corner the date of death of Heinz Hopf's wife Anja as nineteen sixty-something.  Also, there were SmartMatches for Beate and Hedwig Huf, the two 
3 CONC girls on the lower right.  These showed birthdates of 1961 and 1963 respectively.  I pulled those baby photos off of their MyHeritage profiles.  Then for some reason the SmartMatches have disappeared.  If you or anyone reading thi
3 CONC s understands why that happened and what's going on with that, please let me know.
3 CONT
3 CONT I'd guess "Western Germany" was how Ludwig Brinkmann's address was recorded somewhere in the late 1940s, and that address was noted on this document, which was prepared much later.  
3 CONT
3 CONT Regarding my grandfather, remember these are our grandmothers' relatives - Wilhelm Hopf was great grandmother Pauline's younger brother. 
3 CONT
3 CONT This has the look of a page from the files of someone compiling a family tree.  I don't know my mother or grandparents ever to have been engaged in that.  But what everybody was involved with was reparations, and that was going on
3 CONC  for decades.  So my guess is that this information was being compiled for that purpose.
2 NOTE Wow! That is a spectacular find. THANK YOU. I hope that further research online reveals a bit more about Dr. Brinkmann. The only think you did not note (although i am sure you noticed) is that the data is fairly soon after the war
3 CONC , due to the address being "Western Germany." I have a feeling that this data was collected as part of your grandfather's attempt to locate missing relatives after the war. I believe you have posted other examples of his correspon
3 CONC dence in that regard. 
2 NOTE I stumbled upon this today in a file - it shows eight new relatives whom I've added to the tree.  Notice Heinz Hopf, our eminent mathematician cousin, on the upper right.  The typed portion translates as "further information by:" 
3 CONC  followed by the name Dr. Ludwig Brinckmann, with a Munich address, who would thus appear to to have been the source of the information.  I don't know who was compiling the information or why it ended up in the possession of my mo
3 CONC ther or grandparents.
