1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://daten.digitale-sammlungen.de/bsb00000600/images/index.html?id=00000600&groesser=&fip=193.174.98.30&no=&seite=535.  VI.4.4
2 CONT
2 CONT https://daten.digitale-sammlungen.de/bsb00000600/images/index.html?id=00000600&groesser=&fip=193.174.98.30&no=&seite=536.   VII.4
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Meijendorff_von_Yxkull_nr_74#TAB_26
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Killed by Mahnovists along with his brother and brother-in-law Yakov Koulomzin. Wrote letter to his wife and children prior to his death asking them to forgive his killers As recounted in the biography of the Koulomzin family  (un
2 CONC published) by Sophie Koulomzin (nee Schidlovsky):
2 CONT
2 CONT "Both were immediately arrested by the Makno men and taken to the neighboring village of Krutenki.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the way, Yuri managed to send a verbal message to his mother through a local man who had decided to follow the group of arrested men to see what would happen to them. He had been waiting in a room of the Krutenki estate when th
2 CONC e two brothers Meyendorff were brought in. Yuri asked permission to write a letter to his children and a Makno man gave him a piece of paper and a pen. When Yuri had written the letter, the man stuffed it into his pocket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now the real horror began. The Makno men began to beat up Yuri and kept saying:  "Tell us the truth!" Horrified, Lev exclaimed "What do you want him to tell you?!" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Then they began beating Lev. He died fairly quickly and they resumed beating and stamping Yuri who was still alive. At that point, Makno himself entered the room and Yuri cried out to him. But Makno's only response was: "Shoot him
2 CONC " "Too bad to waste a shot." a man answered, and they continued to beat him, at last finishing Yuri off with one final heavy kick.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that point, the man who had taken Yuri's letter said: "Let's see what he writes." and read aloud what Yuri has written:
2 CONT
2 CONT "My Very Dear Children, Perhaps we shall not see each other again in this life. Forgive me, my darlings. I wanted to give you pleasure by visiting you, but the way it worked out, I am depriving you of your father forever. Let us a
2 CONC ccept God's will do not grieve for me, bear your sorrow patiently and may your lives increase goodness and truth on earth. Never try to revenge my death, neither in deed nor in thought. God allowed it. Pray to Him that He forgive 
2 CONC me all my transgressions. I am not afraid of death and I am going to my Lord to answer for my life in the living hope that He will accept me. l am asking Granny to take care of you. Be good grandchildren to her, comfort her in her
2 CONC  sorrow. I bless you, God keep you and God guide you in all that is good.  Your Father"
2 CONT
2 CONT The man who read aloud Yuri's letter to his children as well as the one he wrote to his mother, did bring them to her. He promised to provide horses for them so that they could leave next morning but this proved to be unnecessary 
2 CONC - the whole area had been liberated by the White Army. The family was free to move to Uman where Yuri's home was at their disposal.
2 CONT
2 CONT First, however, Olga (Koulomzin, nee Meyendorff) had to find the bodies of her husband (Yakov Koulomzin) and brothers. In a horse drawn cart, with her five children around her, they drove to the village of Krutenki. Suddenly 5-yea
2 CONC r old Seraphim called out:  "Look, there are Uncle Yuri and Uncle Lev." The bodies were not buried - just thrown down along the road. Yasha's body was found too, together with the body of the local priest, two nuns and an unknown 
2 CONC man. The priest and the nuns were picked by local people, the unknown body Olga placed in her cart and took to Uman. He proved to be a member of a Roman Catholic community there."
2 CONT
2 CONT (see footnote to this whole event at http://www.geni.com/people/Olga-Fedorovna-Kulomzin/6000000014287163765)
