1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Footnote on the death of her husband and brothers by Makhnovists (see http://www.geni.com/people/Georgi-Fedorovich-von-Meyendorff/6000000014287460326):
2 CONT
2 CONT During their first years of immigration, in Paris (circa 1926) walking one day with her young son Yaroslav at the Jardin des Tuileries in Paris, Olga Fyodorovna saw Nestor Makhno. Makhno had escaped his own likely execution and en
2 CONC ded up in Paris around 1922. Apparently he had a very distinctive massive red face. Coming in her direction, she accosted him asking "Why did you kill my husband?" He fell to his knees in a "земной поклон" [prostrate], to which Ol
2 CONC ga Fyodorovna responded in kind. In what was a very Russian tradition, on his part this action was the asking of forgiveness, on hers it was a sign of acceptance of it.  The two put together: an awareness of the FUTILITY of EVERYT
2 CONC HING that had happened.
2 CONT
