0 @N6000000003216078451@ NOTE See Palgrave Chronicle Vol 7 Part 1 (Spring 2001) for further details of this individual's descendants.
0 @N6000000003216046521@ NOTE Entered the Navy as a Cadet on 23 Jan 1867, when he joined HMS Britannia, Portsmouth and he retired in 1880 with the rank of Lieutenant, although he was acting Commander of the frigate 'Dwarf' shortly before retiring.
1 CONT He was promoted Midshipman on 15 April 1868.  He served on the China station in HMS Juno, a 1462 ton Screw Corvette and then returned to England for leave and SubLieutenant's Courses.  Promoted S/Lt on 14 Oct 1872, with a First Cl
1 CONC ass pass in Seamanship and Second Class passes in Gunnery and Navigation.  After completing his Lieutenant'c Course at HMS Greenwich, he joined HMS Revenge for passage to join HMS Invincible in 1874.  Between 1874 and 1875 he serv
1 CONC ed in HMS Invincible, a 6034 ton double-screw Iron Ship, Armour Plated.  His report stated "I should be glad to have him in any ship I Command."  In September 1874 he bought Wilkinson Naval Sword No. 21443, which  is now used by h
1 CONC is g-g-grandson Lt Cdr Peter Archdale, the fourth generation of Archdale to do so.
1 CONT In July 1875 he joined the Despatch vessel Helicon, for passage to Portsmouth, where he joined HM Yacht Victoria and Albert.  Promoted Lieutenant on 20 August 1875, he served in HMS Sultan, an Iron Screw Ship of 9286 tons and Armo
1 CONC ur Plated in 1876, under command of the then Duke of Edinburgh.    Flying C-in-C's flag, Sultan led the Fleet to Constantinople in the Russo-Greek-Turkey war crisis.
1 CONT Between 1876 and 1879 he also served in HMS Black Prince, the first British Iron Clad, which was launced in response to the French vessel La Gloria.  His final appointment prior from April - November 1979 was as Second in Command 
1 CONC (Commander) of HMS Dwarf, a Double Screw Composite Gun-Vessel of 584 tons that was on the Cape of Good Hope and West Africa Station.
1 CONT The above details were extracted from the Navy Lists of the period and also information provided by EFA from EMA's papers.
1 CONT
1 CONT He farmed Riversdale, to which he succeeded on the death of his uncle William Humphrys Mervyn Archdall in June 1889.  He also had large farms at Rossahilly and Crocknacrieve - almost 1000 acres.  His original estate was 5,300 acre
1 CONC s and he was one of the first landlords to sell to his tenants.   He moved to his late uncle's house at Riversdale in 1897, but during the five years 1898-1903 while he represented North Fermanagh, he rented a house in Warwick Squ
1 CONC are for his family.  During his later service as an MP, he also maintained a house in 36 Belgrave Road, SW.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sold Crocknacrieve and about 250 acres of land to John West, uncle of Harry West (Lord Erne), for £2000 who in turn sold it to the Loanes in 1921.  John West then bought Rossahilly.
1 CONT
1 CONT He became a Magistrate and Deputy Lieutenant for Fermanagh, was High Sheriff of the county in 1884 and acquired the Riversdale property from his uncle William.  He sat in Parliament for North Fermanagh, as a Unionist, from Apr 189
1 CONC 7 to 1903 and again from 1916-1922, for the same county, being made a Privy Councillor on 7 Mar 1921, and Minister of Agriculture and Minister of Commerce in the Ulster Parliament in 1921-33.  Imperial Grand Master of the Loyal Or
1 CONC ange Institution from 1926 to 1937 and Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of Ireland from 1924 to 1940.  President of Belfast Chamber of Commerce 1929.  In 1926 Queen's University Belfast awarded him the honorary degree of LL.D.
1 CONT Created Baronet 25 June 1928.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a keen sportsman, his recreations including hunting, yachting, golf and shooting.  He was Commodore of the Enniskillen Yacht Club and President of the Enniskillen Golf Club.
1 CONT
1 CONT Probate granted to Nicholas Edward Archdale and Lt Col Henry Sacheverell Carelton Richardson.
1 CONT
1 CONT MINISTRY OF FINANCE FOR NORTHERN IRELAND.
1 CONT ESTATE OF EDWARD ARCHDALE.
1 CONT Record No. E.G. 259. Co. Fermanagh.
1 CONT TOWNLANDS OF FORMIL, PROCKLIS, GORLAVE, TULLYNALOOB, GREAGHMORE, GLENVANNON, AND PART OF DERRYNASHESK
1 CONT ESTATE OF E. M. ARCHDALE.
1 CONT Record No. E 835. Co. Fermanagh.
1 CONT TOWNLANDS OF DROMORE BIG AND DROMORE LITTLE.
1 CONT The Ministry of Finance for Northern Ireland hereby gives notice that, pursuant to the Provisions
1 CONT of the Land Purchase Acts, it is prepared to consider offers for the disposal by Lease of the exclusive right of mining and taking and digging and searching for certain minerals, namely Coal,Lignite, Lime, Iron Ore, Bauxite, Pipe 
1 CONC Clay, Kaolin or China Clay, Quartz and Silica, Spar, Silicified Wood, Platinum, Diatomite and Copper, in the above lands.  Offers stating the Dead Rent, Royalties, and term proposed, and giving three references, must be received a
1 CONC t the address below on or before the 22nd instant.
1 CONT A map illustrating the lands can be inspected in the office of the Ministry (Room 268) during office hours. Forms of application can be obtained from the Secretary, Ministry of Finance, Stormont,
1 CONT The Lease to be granted will contain the usual covenants and conditions in similar leases by the Ministry.
1 CONT Dated this 10th day of November, 1943.
1 CONT C. H. PETHERICK,
1 CONT Assistant Secretary, Ministry of Finance, Stormont, Belfast
1 CONT (Bel Gazette 12 Nov 1943)
1 CONT SIR EDWARD ARCHDALE
1 CONT FARMING IN NORTHERN IRELAND
1 CONT The Right Hon. Sir Edward Archdale, Bt., first Minister of Agriculture for Northern Ireland, and one of the four remaining members of the first Ulster Cabinet, has died at his home at Balliniamallard, Co. Fermanagh, at the age of 
1 CONC 90. Edward Mervyn Archdale, eldest son of the late Nicholas Montgomery Atchdale, of Crock na Crieve, County Fermanagh, was born on Januaryr 26,1853,.and educated at:the Naval School, Portsmouth. He entered the Navy in 1866, was pr
1 CONC omoted Lieutenant in 1875, and retired in 1880 after having served on the China and Mediterranean stations and in African waters., In 1884 he was.elected High Sheriff of Fermanagh, and from 1898 to 1903, and again frorn 1916 to 19
1 CONC 21 he sat at Westminster as Conservative M.P. for North Fermanagh. In the latter year he was appointed Minister of Agriculture and Cominerce in the first Government formed by the then Sir James Craig; he held this office until 192
1 CONC 5, and continued as Minister of Agriculture frorn 1925 to 1933. His experience as a landlord and.his wide practical knowledge of farming made him an admirable choice for the post. In the Northern Ireland Parliament Sir Edward Arch
1 CONC dale, who was created a baronet in 1928, sat for Fermanagh.and Tyrone, 1921- 29, and for the Enniskillen Division 1929-37. A keen sportsman; he was extremely popular in County Fermanagh, and indeed throughout Northern Ireland and 
1 CONC Lord Craigavon referred to him on his retirement.in 1'937 as "Ulster's Grand Old Man."  He had been chairman of the standing committee of the Ulster Unionist Council since its establishment in 1905.  He married in 1880 Alicia Blan
1 CONC d, daughter of the late Quintin Fleming of Chapelville, Liverpool. They had five sons, one of whorm was killed in the last war, and a daughter. His wife died inr 1924. The heir to the baronetcy is Vice-Admiral N. E.- Archdale, C.B
1 CONC .E,. who was born in 1881, is' married, and has a spn ,and a daughter.  (The Times, 3 Nov 1943)
0 @N6000000003216049763@ NOTE Joined the Navy in 1894 and trained at HMS Britannia until 1896, where he passed out sixth overall and was promoted Midshipman in 15 Jan 1897 to join the 14,900 ton Twin-screw Armoured Battleship HMS Majestic, Flagship of the Chan
1 CONC nel Squadron and was present at the Diamond Jubilee Spithead Review 26 June 1897.  On 13 Jan 1898 he joined HMS Caesar, a Portsmouth-based Battleship of the same class as Majestic.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Gunnery Courses at HMS Excellent, Portsmouth, he was promoted Lieutenant on 15 Jan 1902.  During 1902 and 1903 he served in HMS Hazard, a Twin Screw Torpedo Gunboat of 1070 tons, for "Special Service" and HMS Thames, a Twin 
1 CONC Screw Cruiser, Second Class of 4050 tons, "additional for submarine boats".
1 CONT Joined Helicon on 12 Nov 1903 and then HMS Vernon, additional for Torpedo Duties.  Vernon was 5481 tons and the Torpedo School Ship (late HMS Donegal, which had been built in Devonport in 1858).  On 11 Jan 1906 he joined HMS Iphig
1 CONC enia "for Special Service", She was a Twin Screw Protected Cruiser, 2nd Class of 3600 tons, that Commissioned in Portsmouth on 30 Jan 1906.  In 1907 he served in HMS Sapphire, a Twin Screw Protected Cruiser, 3rd Class of 3000 tons
1 CONC  that was Flagship of Rear Admiral (D) Commanding Torpedo and Submarine Craft in Commission.
1 CONT
1 CONT He joined Submarine Depot Ship Forth on 11 Nov 1908 as Second in Command, before taking command of the submarine depot ship HMS Rosario and the Hong Kong Submarine Flotilla in 1911.  Promoted Cdr 30 Jun 1913.  Appointed to HMS For
1 CONC th 31 Oct 1914, in command and for duty with submarines.  Mentioned in Despatches in recognition of distinguished services during the War and his name was published in the London Gazette dated 13 April 1915 after operations off th
1 CONC e Belgian coast in HMS Hazard.  Promoted Captain 30 Jun 1918 and took command of HMS Vulcan, a Special Torpedo Vessel Depot Ship.  Based at Chatham, he was also in command of the Torpedo Boat Flotilla.
1 CONT Appointed to HMS Greenwich 28 Feb 1919 for Senior Officer's Technical Course and afterwards to HMS Vernon in command of the Naval Mining School.
1 CONT Appointed on 17 Mar 1920, Commander of the Military Division of the Most Excellent Order of the British Empire, "for valuable services in command of HMS Greenwich, and as Senior Naval Officer Copenhagen".
1 CONT Promoted Rear Admiral 7 Oct 1929  (L Gazette 8 Oct 1929)
1 CONT Promoted Vice Admiral (Retd) 16 Jan 1935 (L Gazette 18 Jan 1935)
1 CONT General Inspector Ministry of Home Affairs NI 1931-46 and Civil Defence Officer for Derry.
1 CONT On inheriting the estate of Riversdale on the death of his father in 1943, Sir Nicholas felt he was unable to maintain the property and so sold it to the N Ireland Ministry of Agriculture in 1947, who subsequently pulled down the 
1 CONC house in the 1960's, but kept the stables for offices and general storage.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Burke's Guide to Country Houses of Ireland Vol. 1: Ireland by Mark Bence-Jones, Crocknacrieve was built by Captain John Johnston, whose widow married H. M. Richardson of Rossfad; who, when he inherited the latter esta
1 CONC te, and part of Rich Hill (an important C17 house, built between 1664 and 1690 by Major Edward Richardson, MP), handed Crocknacrieve over to his cousin Nicholas Archdale, who added a wing, said to have been built from stone from t
1 CONC he old Folliott castle at Ballinamallard. Sir Edward sold the property in 1901; in 1921 it was bought by S. C. Loane, whose wife (nee Barton) was the grand-daughter of H. M. Richardson.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vice-Admiral Sir Edward Archdale, Bt., C.B.E., died at Crowborough, Sussex, on Thursday at the age of 74. Nicholas Edward Archdale was born in 1881, the eldest son of Sir Edward Archdale, first baronet, Minister of Agriculture for
1 CONC  Northern Ireland from 1921 to 1933. He was educated at the Royal Naval Academy, Gosport, and at H.M.S. Britannia. He entered the Navy as a midshipman fn 1897 and joined the submarine service in 1902. He specialized in torpedoes a
1 CONC nd was promoted to Commander in 1913 after two years in command of the submarine flotilla at Hong Kong. In the early years of the First World War he commanded flotillas attached to the Forth and the Hazard, and in the latter ship 
1 CONC took part in bombardments of German positions on the Belgian coast. In 1918 he was promoted captain when in command of the flotilla attached to the Vulcan. He was appointed C.B.E. in 1920 for his services in command of the depot s
1 CONC hip Greenwich and as senior naval officer at Copenhagen during the operations in the Baltic. From 1921 for two years he was head of the mining department of the Vernon, and afterwards commanded the cruiser Ceres in the Mediterrane
1 CONC an. He returned to be Captain of the Dockyard and King's Harbour Master at Rosyth in 1925, and after commanding the battleship Malaya he was promoted to flag rank in 1929 and in the same year was naval A.D.C. to the King. He retir
1 CONC ed in 1930, and for the next 16 years was general inspector under the Ministry of Home Affairs, Northern Ireland. He was promoted vice-admiral on the retired list in 1935, and succeeded his father as second baronet in 1943. He mar
1 CONC ried, in 1920, Gerda, daughter of the late F. C. Sievers, of Copenhagen, who survives him. There were a son and a daughter of ihe marriage. The son Commander Edward Folmer Archdale, DSC., succeeds to the baronetcy. He is 34 and la
1 CONC st year married Miss Elizabeth Ann Stewart, daughter of Major-General Wilfrid F. Lukis, C.B.E. (from Times obit dated 1 Aug 1955))
0 @N6000000003216050333@ NOTE Attended St Helen's School. Pembroke Road, Dublin (at the same time as his younger brother Audley) where he won a prize for drawing.
1 CONT The Irish Farmers' Gazette 11 April 1903 stated; "An Irish student in Mr W P Archdale of Riversdale, County Fermanagh, was among the successful candidates at the examinations with which the winter session at the West of Scotland A
1 CONC gricultural College was brought to a close at Glasgow last week.  Mr Archdale secured special certificates in the senior courses on practical Agriculture with soils and manures, he was also among the prize takers in botany.  Mr Ar
1 CONC chdale, we understand, has concluded his two years course, and was on a Scotch farm last summer."
1 CONT Served in the North of Ireland Imperial Yeomanry in 1903 and 1904.  Resigned his commission and sailed for Egypt during Jan 1905 on the SS Nitocris, arriving in Alexandria on to work for an agricultural company.
1 CONT In Constantia during May 1905.  In Behera in 1906 and Constantia in 1908.  Critically ill with pleuro-pneumonia at Dahr-Sounna(?) in Dec 1907 (mentioned in a letter from Verschoyle to his mother).
1 CONT Served with the Sudan Cotton Plantation Syndicate between 1911-1945.  CBE 2 Jun 1943.  The Gezira project originated in 1911, when a private British enterprise, Sudan Plantations Syndicate, found cotton suited to the area and emba
1 CONC rked on what in the 1920s became the Gezira Scheme, intended principally to furnish cotton to the British textile industry. Backed by a loan from the British government, the syndicate began a dam on the Blue Nile at Sannar in 1913
1 CONC . Work was interrupted by World War I, and the dam was not completed until 1925. The project was limited by a 1929 agreement between Sudan and Egypt that restricted the amount of water Anglo-Egyptian Sudan could use during the dry
1 CONC  season. By 1931 the project had expanded to 450,000 hectares, the maximum that then could be irrigated by the available water, although 10,000 more hectares were added in the 1950s. The project was nationalized in 1950, and was o
1 CONC perated by the Sudan Gezira Board as a government enterprise.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1913 Zeidab.  Served as a Temporary Captain during the Darfur Operation in 1916 and specially mentioned for his service for "Lines of Communication".  Later in 1916 he was working in Barakat, Sudan.  His engagement to Alice Talbot
1 CONC  was announced on 1 July 1917.  He was working in Jajiba, Sudan in August 1918.  Returned to Riversdale, Ballinamallard, Fermanagh from Sudan for leave in August 1920, via Alexandria and Genoa, then overland and sailed from London
1 CONC  to Port Said, arriving 5 Nov 1920.  His next leave to Riversdale was August 1922, returning to Sudan in November of that year.
1 CONT
1 CONT There are two references to him, on pages 100 and 105 in Arthur Gaitskell's 1959 book "Gezira: A Story of Development in the Sudan".  The paragraph on page 105 states that "The Assistant Manager, Archdale, was a son of Sir Edward 
1 CONC Archdale, Bt., for many years Minister of Agriculture in Northern Ireland.  He had learned farming the hard way working as a young man with cow-men and shepherds.  Later he too worked in a land company in Egypt and transferred to 
1 CONC Zeidab.  He brought to the Gezira some of the quality of his homeland which has bred so many great soldiers.  Archdale was restless, energetic and contemptuous of all obstacles.  He spoke Arabic like a native, was a born leader on
1 CONC  the outside job and carried staff and tenants with him in impulsive enthusiasm.  He too had pertinent advice to give.  Confronted with a complexity of courses men would turn to him.  'Do the difficult thing,' he would say. 'It is
1 CONC  almost sure to be right'".
1 CONT
1 CONT From 'The Sudan Star', May 9, 1946.
1 CONT "W. P. Archdale, Manager of the Sudan Plantations Syndicate has left the Sudan on retirement, as already announced, a friend and fellow worker writes:
1 CONT "Last week Billy Archdale slipped away quietly out of the country after thirty years service to it, and with him went the end of an Epoch.  He was one of the last Englishmen of the paternal age, one of the band of men whose strong
1 CONC  and vivid personalities created the first order and prosperity this country had ever known.  Coming up from Egypt as long as 1910 he became one of the pioneers of the Gezira Scheme, and, par excellence, the tenants' man.  A count
1 CONC ryman himself, (his father Sir Edward Archdale, Bart, was for many years Minister of Agriculture in Northern Ireland) he had a natural sympathy with the countrymen of the Gezira, tempered with a very shrewd knowledge of their weak
1 CONC nesses.  An unusual mastery of the spoken Arabic Language and an untiring energy made him quickly their confidant and mentor.  His name Archdale became a household word, transcribed as "Ershed", their teacher.  The Sudan will alwa
1 CONC ys owe him a debt for building into the foundations of its most important economic venture a tradition of hard work, human intimacy, and intolerance of shoddy standards. "
1 CONT Sportsman And Naturalist
1 CONT "He was a great sportsman and naturalist, delighting in birds, fishes and animals of all kinds.  Out of thousands of wild bulls which passed through his hands for training, he would easily remember an individual animal seen two ye
1 CONC ars later pulling a plough in a distant tenancy.  At home in Northern Ireland he had astonishing success in training shooting dogs.  Here in the Sudan he was famous as a trainer of polo ponies.  Under his hands nervous ponies beca
1 CONC me quiet yet ready to jump forward like an arrow.  All animals had an instinctive respect and trust for him.  And not animals only.  Probably the most lasting picture of him is as a polo captain.  A superb player in his prime, his
1 CONC  mere presence on the side was often the cause of victory, for he made men play above their usual form."
1 CONT Right Without Logic
1 CONT "As a man, a true Irishman, he was prepared to argue what at times seemed black was white, and embarrassed accurate minds by his lack of care for logic, but he had an uncanny knack of being right in essential human values.  Of all
1 CONC  his qualities, his loyalty was perhaps the most outstanding, but it was a loyalty to persons, not to things, the sort of loyalty that sticks to a friend be he right or wrong.  This very trait isolated him in some ways from younge
1 CONC r generations, for he had little use for the theories which much more than persons, claim the loyalties of modern minds.  He had a contempt for shams and falsehood and a distrust of ease and comfort, keeping himself hard and fit f
1 CONC or any duty that might come his way.  To those in trouble, unable to make up their minds whether to take the high road or the low, he would give advice "Do the difficult thing," he would say, "it is almost certain to be right"."
1 CONT C.B.E. A Token Tribute
1 CONT "A profound sense of duty dominated his actions.  This respect for discipline kept him on his job in this country without honour in the first world war and cast a shadow over his whole life, for he was born to be a leader of fight
1 CONC ing men and every instinct in his nature called him into war.  It kept him also, often a lonely and weary man, on his job throughout this war long after his retiring age."
1 CONT "He had a rare innate modesty which made some think him unsociable, it was typical of his character to think himself unfit to be given the C.B.E. and only to accept it as a token tribute to his staff.  Although he belonged to an a
1 CONC uthoritative age, and so had little sympathy with certain modern trends; his virtues, courage, energy, loyalty."
1 CONT Bought Mountfield Lodge with 100 acres of land and 40,000 acres of shooting from Sir Lionel McMahon in 1924/5 and sold it to General Dennis Moore in 1947, after which he retired to Natal.
1 CONT
1 CONT CBE 1943.
