0 @N6000000003216067204@ NOTE Served in the 14th Hussars from 1860 to 1865, reaching the rank of Captain.  In 1885 he was appointed Consul for the Society Islands and resided at Tahiti.  Transferred to Taganrog as Consul for several Governments in the Russian 
1 CONC Empire in 1890 and went to Corunna five years later.  Appointed Consul General at Boston, USA in 1902.
0 @N6000000003216067170@ NOTE The following points were gleaned from Alice's diaries for the period 1917-1925
1 CONT Attended a munitions manufacturing course at Shoreditch Technical Institute with her sister Geraldine on 20 Feb 1917.  They both had to undergo a "medical" to ensure they were strong enough for the work required.  Geraldine did no
1 CONC t pass the exam and on 2 March 1917 they both left the course.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 6 Dec 1918 travelled aboard the Hospital ship S.S. Neutralia from Liverpool, arriving in Alexandria 18 December, Luxor 20 December and Barakat on 25 December.  Her return journey was from Khartoum on 9 June 1919 and Port Sudan 
1 CONC on 10 June.  Sailed on the S.S. 'Umona', reaching Suez on 14 June, Gibraltar on 22 June and Tilbury on the 29th.
1 CONT
1 CONT Moved into Arnisfield, (or Laragh, as it was known) which was owned by the Lyons, on 4 Jul 1919 and then to her father-in-law's house at Riversdale on 23 Jul 1920, in anticipation of husband Billy's return from the Sudan on leave.
1 CONT
1 CONT She travelled on the S.S. Caledonia, leaving from London on 21 Sep1921 to Alexandria and thence to Barakat, arriving on 9 Oct 1921.  Her daughter Angel was left in London in the care of her sister Geraldine Chetwynd-Talbot.  Her r
1 CONC eturn journey started on 7 Mar 1922 on the S.S. Dunvegan Castle from Port Sudan to Marseilles, then by train via Paris and ferry to London, arriving on 22 Mar.  For the final leg of her homeward journey she travelled on 26 Mar fro
1 CONC m Euston to Fleetwood, then ferry to Belfast and train to Ballinamallard.
1 CONT
1 CONT For her next journey to Barakat, she travelling with her sister-in-law Angel Tottenham, arriving in October 1923.
1 CONT
1 CONT When her husband moved to South Africa, Alice moved from N. Ireland to London.  She took a flat in Hurlingham Court, Putney.  Alice lived there until all her children were married and then lived with each of them for a few months 
1 CONC at a time.
