0 @N6000000003216137885@ NOTE In 1895 he began 42 years service with the Egyptian Government as an Irrigation Engineer with the principal task of harnassing the waters of the Nile and the Sudan.  Founded Malakal, later capital of the Upper Nile Province, in 19
1 CONC 05 as a dockyard for a proposed dredging fleet.  Inspector-General of Irrigation Sudan 1909-19, a member of the Governor-General's council 1910-13.  Initiated the project of building the Blue Nile Dam and the irrigation of 5 milli
1 CONC on acres of Gezira plain.  Inspector General of Irrigation Lower Egypt 1914-16 and of Public Works 1917-19.  Under Secretary of State for Public Works 1919-25, (headed the mission to the Uganda Government in 1923 to examine the so
1 CONC urce of the White Nile).  Agent and Chief Inspecting Engineer to Egyptian Government in London 1925-37.
1 CONT Created CBE in 1919 for valuable services in Egypt during the war.  Grand Cordon Nile Order of Ismail, Medjidie and Osmanich.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr Percy Tottenhain, CBE, who died on Monday at the age of 101, was perhaps the last of the distinguished British civil engineers Who served the Egyptian Government from 1885 to 1925 (Egypt having attained Independence in 1922), e
1 CONC ither as Adviser of Under-Secretary of State to the Ministry of Public Works. Over these 40 years their fame rests mainly on the irrigation works for control of the waters of the Nile in Egypt and Sudan. Percy Marmaduke Tottenham,
1 CONC  born in Ireland in 1873, educated at Repton and the Royal Indian Engineering College at Coopers Hill, began 42 years of service with the Egyptian Government in 1895 in the Irrigation Department of the Public Works Ministry. In 19
1 CONC 04, on the creation of an irrigation branch of the Egyptian Government in Sudan, with C. E. Dupuis as Inspector General, Tottenham accompanied him, as his deputy on the Sobat to Bor expedition through 200 miles of unexplored count
1 CONC ry, to examine the line of a possible Jongidi canal. In 1905 Tottenhiam founded and named Malakal on the White Nile to be the dockyard for thie proposed dredging fleet of the Egyptian Government and this site later became the capi
1 CONC tal of the Upper Nile Province of the Sudan Government. In 1909.  On the promotion of Dupuis as Under-Secretary of State to the Public Works Ministry, he was appointed Inspector General, and in 1910 was made a member of the Counci
1 CONC l of the Governor-General of Sudan. That "the Gezira Canial may be regarded as the great hope of Sudan" had been foretold by Dupuis, whose scheme involved construction of a high dam across the Blue Nile. For this Tottenham found a
1 CONC  suitable site, in 1911, at Makvar, near Sennar, and he then developed the proiect for the dam and canalization, thus completing his senior's vision for irrigation of about five mil!ion acres of the Gezira Plain. Tottenham also pr
1 CONC esented for approval of both governments an ambitious project, suggested in 1907 by Sir William Willcocks, for construction of a dam across the White Nile at Gebel Auliya, the tail-reach to serve as a reservoir and so provide Egyp
1 CONC t with the quickest and cheapest means of increasing her water supply. Both projects were approved by the twvo governments in 1914, but because of war and other causes their completion was delayed until 1925 and 1937 respectively.
1 CONC  Tottenham's appointment as Inspector General of Irrigation for Lowver Egypt followed in 1914. During the First World War he deputized as Under-Secretary of State for Public Worlks, while the then Under- Secretary, Sir Murdoch Mac
1 CONC Donald, was employed on the Suez Canal. In 1918 he was created CBE, "for valuable services during the war ". In 1919 he succeeded to the post of Under-Secretary of State for Public Works, and to round off his knowledge of the Nile
1 CONC  and its basin, he left Cairo in 1923 as head of a mission to the Uganda Government to examine the source of the White Nile in the Equatorial Lakes. In the preceding year, 1922, fhe Tutankhamun tomb was discovered and, as Tottenha
1 CONC m's responsibilities included the Antiquities Department. he and the French Director General of that department were amonig the earliest to be invited to view the antechamber. For the final 12 years of his seivice with the Egyptia
1 CONC n Government, Tottenham was made its Chief Inspecting Engineer in London (with a branch office in Brussels), responsible for all commelcial and military equipment purchased in Euirope and the United Kingdonm. In 1909 he married An
1 CONC gel, only daughter of Sii- Edward Archdale, Bt. former Minister of Agriculture in Northern Ireland. They had three sots and a daughter.  (The Times, London, 31 Jan 1975)
